 ## Overview
-Marching Cubes 33 (MC33) is an algorithm for constructing isosurfaces from volumetric datasets on a cuberille grid that resolves the topological ambiguities inherent in the original 15-configuration Marching Cubes method [S1]. It processes each cell independently to produce a triangle model whose topology coincides exactly with the topology of the trilinear function defining the isosurface [S1]. Unlike its sibling [Extended Trilinear Triangulation](1618-extended-trilinear-triangulation.md), which uses convex hulls of intersection points, MC33 relies on an expanded lookup table of 33 entries derived from analytical tests for face and internal connectivity [S1].
+Marching Cubes 33 (MC33) is an algorithm for constructing isosurfaces from volumetric datasets on a cuberille grid that resolves the topological ambiguities inherent in the original 15-configuration Marching Cubes method [S1]. It processes each cell independently to produce a triangle model whose topology coincides exactly with the topology of the trilinear function defining the isosurface [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A scalar field sampled on a cubic grid; a specific cube cell defined by 8 corner node values $F_{000}, \dots, F_{111}$; and an isovalue (typically 0).
-*   **Output:** A set of triangles representing the isosurface within that cube.
+*   **Input:** A scalar field sampled on a cubic grid; a specific cube cell defined by 8 corner node values $F_{000}, \dots, F_{111}$; and an isovalue (typically 0) [S1].
